  "derived": "2026-10-01, from the cached workbook at sim/cache/ofgem_cap_level_models/ (gitignored). The electricity column is independently carried by ofgem_cap_unit_rate_composition.json `standing_charge_p_per_day_ex_vat`, and tests/simulation/test_standing_charge_reproduces_the_cap_model.py asserts the two agree.",
  "version_token_is": "in_filename_only",
  "how_to_recheck": "THE URL ABOVE IS A VERSION HISTORY PAGE, NOT THE WORKBOOK, and it is NOT the page this artefact cited until 2026-09-07. That one (/energy-policy-and-regulation/policy-and-regulatory-programmes/default-tariff-cap) returns 200 and carries exactly one .xlsx link -- the Annex 9 levelisation workbook -- so the old recipe, 'find the cap level model link there', could never terminate in anything but cannot_tell. A 200 serving nothing on the subject reads exactly like a working check. GET the URL above and take the HIGHEST v<major>.<minor> across every link matching Default[-_]tariff[-_]cap[-_]level OR Energy-price-cap-levels-pre-levelised-rates-model: the series is published under BOTH names (v1.30 under the second), so a check keyed to the filename stem rather than to the version number misses editions. If the highest is above `version_token`, the standing charges here were derived from a superseded model.",
  "checked_for_supersession": {
   "on": "2026-09-07",
   "found": "current",
   "note": "INHERITED, NOT RE-ASKED. This artefact was derived on 2026-10-01 from the SAME workbook ofgem_cap_unit_rate_composition.json reads, and that artefact asked the supersession question of the publisher on 2026-09-07 and found v1.31 current. Nobody asked it again on 2026-10-01; the date above is when it was last asked, which is the honest age of the answer."
  }
